          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Grace is God’s freely given, undeserved favor toward sinners, especially in salvation through Christ; it may also be understood as an active power that transforms and enables godly living.\",\"book_summary\":\"Grace is initiating divine favor that both saves and actively teaches its recipient to reject ungodliness; alleged grace that licenses continued lawlessness is counterfeit.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"God’s unmerited saving favor or gift toward undeserving people.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"God’s initiating favor understood as necessarily continuing in morally formative instruction toward righteous living.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book makes grace’s continuing opposition to ungodliness an authenticity boundary. It excludes or reclassifies as counterfeit any purported grace construed as permission to remain lawless, and it regards a definition stopping at saving favor as materially incomplete. The consensus recognizes transforming grace but does not uniformly make that function part of the minimum identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction with the consensus’s central identification of grace as unmerited divine favor. The disagreement concerns whether transformative moral instruction is constitutive of authentic grace rather than a common effect or secondary aspect.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the established referent—God’s prior, undeserved favor—but adds a necessary ethical and pedagogical condition. This is a refinement rather than divergence or novelty because several consensus responses already identify grace as transforming power and cite Titus 2:11–12. The material change is the book’s reclassification of permissive or non-transformative conceptions as counterfeit.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The entry could be classified as MATCH because mainstream consensus does not actually teach that grace permits lawlessness and already recognizes Titus 2:11–12 as showing grace’s transforming work. However, the book elevates that work from an acknowledged effect or nuance to a defining boundary, making REFINED the better classification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
